# SYNOPSIS

```c
vx_status vx_irq_create(vx_handle resource, uint32_t line, vx_handle *out);
vx_status vx_irq_create_msi(vx_handle resource, uint32_t source,
                            vx_handle *out, vx_msi *msi);
vx_status vx_irq_ack(vx_handle irq);
vx_status vx_iorange_create(vx_handle resource, uint16_t base, uint32_t count,
                            vx_handle *out);
```

# DESCRIPTION

Drivers run in user space (see the kernel's design, 01 §7).
What they need of the hardware the kernel gives them as objects: device memory as physical VMOs (`vmo_create`), DMA through domains (dma(2)), and the interrupts and I/O ports this page describes.
Each is made from a Resource, the root authority over device space, whose handle needs MANAGE; the kernel gives it to the root task, and devmgr and svcd mint each driver's objects from it and give the driver handles to them.

`vx_irq_create` makes an Irq for an interrupt line.
On x86_64 <line> is an ISA IRQ below 16, through the firmware's overrides, or a GSI, routed through the IOAPICs to the boot CPU; ISA lines are edge-triggered and the rest level-triggered, unless an override says otherwise.
On aarch64 it is a GIC SPI's INTID, 32 or more; SGIs and PPIs are the kernel's.
A line has at most one Irq; once the Irq's last handle goes, the line is masked and free again.

`vx_irq_create_msi` makes an Irq for an MSI or MSI-X interrupt of the PCI function whose requester ID (`bus << 8 | device << 3 | function`) is <source>.
The kernel picks the interrupt and writes into <msi> what the device must write to raise it: `data` at `address`.
On x86_64 it is a local-APIC vector on the boot CPU; on aarch64 an LPI through the GIC's ITS, which knows the device by its requester ID and is set up the first time an MSI is made.
A function may have several, each with its own `data`.
Both wrappers make the one call `irq_create`, the second with `VX_IRQ_MSI`.

An Irq handle has WAIT, WRITE, DUPLICATE, TRANSFER and INSPECT.
A driver binds it to a port with `VX_TRIGGER_IRQ` (`port_bind`, which needs WAIT); when the line fires, the kernel counts it, and the packet's value is the count since the Irq was made.
A binding is one-shot: if the line fires with no binding, the kernel remembers, and the next binding fires at once.
A level-triggered line is masked when it fires, since it would fire again at once, and stays masked until `vx_irq_ack`.
An edge-triggered line, and every MSI, is never masked, because an edge that came while it was masked would be lost, and the device with it; the driver therefore handles everything the device has pending before it binds again.

`vx_irq_ack` unmasks the line of <irq>, which needs WRITE, if the kernel masked it; otherwise it does nothing.

`vx_iorange_create` makes an IoRange for the <count> I/O ports from <base>, at most up to port `0xffff`; x86_64 alone has them.
Its handle has MAP, DUPLICATE, TRANSFER and INSPECT.
A task uses the ports once `as_map` has been called with the task's handle (MANAGE), the IoRange (MAP) in place of a VMO, and the offset, size and flags 0; its threads may then use `in` and `out` on them, the calling thread at once and the others from their next switch onto a CPU.
A task has room for 32 ranges.

The kernel's console is the kernel's until a driver is given its device: an IoRange covering COM1 on x86_64, or a physical VMO over the PL011 on aarch64, takes it, and from then on the kernel writes there only to report a panic, unless `vx.kconsole` is on the command line.

# EXAMPLES

From the kernel's tests (`tests/kernel/ktest.c`), on x86_64, where line 3 is COM2's, which nothing uses:

```c
CHECK(vx_irq_create(res, SPARE_LINE, &h) == VX_OK);
CHECK(vx_irq_create(res, SPARE_LINE, &h2) == VX_ERR_EXISTS); // one Irq a line
vx_handle port;
vx_packet pk;
CHECK(vx_port_create(0, &port) == VX_OK);
CHECK(vx_port_bind(port, h, VX_TRIGGER_COUNTER_GE, 1, 1) == VX_ERR_INVALID);
CHECK(vx_port_bind(port, h, VX_TRIGGER_IRQ, 1, 0) == VX_OK);
```

and COM2's ports:

```c
CHECK(vx_iorange_create(res, 0x2f8, 8, &h) == VX_OK); // COM2's ports
CHECK(vx_as_map(self, h, 0, 4096, 0, &at) == VX_ERR_INVALID);
CHECK(vx_as_map(self, h, 0, 0, 0, &at) == VX_OK);
```

